 ## Subtopics
 
+### [Face Visibility Check](3701-face-visibility-check.md)
+Face Visibility Check determines whether a specific voxel face needs to be meshed based on its own visibility state, the neighbor's visibility state, and whether the face has already been processed. It solves the problem of redundant geometry generation by culling hidden faces between two opaque full cubes. Unlike complex partial-shape culling which checks area coverage, this method relies on boolean opacity states to decide if a face is visible.
+
 ### Quad Data Structure
-Represents a merged rectangular face with axis, direction, slice, position, dimensions, and material. It is the output format for naive, culled, and greedy meshing, allowing comparison of the generated geometry sets [S1]. The `makeQuad` function returns an object with fields: `axis` (0|1|2), `dir` (1|-1), `slice` (number), `u` (number), `w` (number), `v` (number), `h` (number), `material` (number) [S1]. The triangle indices for a quad depend on `dir`: if `dir=1`, indices are `[0,1,2,0,2,3]`; if `dir=-1`, indices are `[0,3,2,0,2,1]` [S1]. The axes `u` and `v` are derived from `axis` using `AXIS_U = [1, 2, 0]` and `AXIS_V = [2, 0, 1]`, ensuring the cross product $\hat{u} \times \hat{v}$ points in the `axis` direction [S1].
+Represents a merged rectangular face with axis, direction, slice, position, dimensions, and material [S1]. It is the output format for naive, culled, and greedy meshing, allowing comparison of the generated geometry sets [S1]. The `makeQuad` function returns an object with fields: `axis` (0|1|2), `dir` (1|-1), `slice` (number), `u` (number), `w` (number), `v` (number), `h` (number), `material` (number) [S1]. The triangle indices for a quad depend on `dir`: if `dir=1`, indices are `[0,1,2,0,2,3]`; if `dir=-1`, indices are `[0,3,2,0,2,1]` [S1]. The axes `u` and `v` are derived from `axis` using `AXIS_U = [1, 2, 0]` and `AXIS_V = [2, 0, 1]`, ensuring the cross product $\hat{u} \times \hat{v}$ points in the `axis` direction [S1].
 
 ### Culled Voxel Meshing
-This variant omits faces that are adjacent to another solid voxel. It generates geometry for voxels but culls faces based on a boundary policy (`"air"` or `"solid"`) [S1]. On a solid $32^3$ cube, it produces 6,144 quads. On a $32^3$ terrain chunk with 17,191 solid voxels, it produces 6,654 quads, 26,616 vertices, and 13,308 triangles [S1]. It reduces geometry compared to naive meshing (6,654 vs 103,146 quads) but produces more than greedy meshing (6,654 vs 2,416 quads) on the same terrain chunk [S1]. Performance on an Apple M2 Pro is approximately 2.06 ms median time for the terrain chunk [S1].
