+
+### Validator Expected Faces Generation
+Used to verify meshing correctness by generating a sorted list of expected face tokens. It iterates through every voxel, checks all 6 directions for empty neighbors, and constructs a 64-bit token encoding axis, direction, depth, u, v, and material [S5, unofficial]. The tokens are sorted and returned to allow exact set-equality comparisons against the mesher's output [S5, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-**Time Complexity:** $O(N)$, where $N$ is the number of voxels in the chunk. Each voxel is visited once, and for each solid voxel, 6 neighbor checks are performed [S1, S3].
-**Memory Complexity:** $O(F)$, where $F$ is the number of visible faces. The output buffer stores one quad per visible face [S3].
+**Time Complexity:** $O(N)$, where $N$ is the number of voxels in the chunk. Each voxel is visited once, and for each solid voxel, 6 neighbor checks are performed [S1, S3, unofficial].
+**Memory Complexity:** $O(F)$, where $F$ is the number of visible faces. The output buffer stores one quad per visible face [S3, unofficial].
 **Performance:** Visible Block Faces is very fast, generating about 40 million quads per second on a single core of a 2.5 GHz Intel Core i7 [S3, S4]. It is significantly faster than Greedy Meshing, which takes about 3 times longer to produce a more optimal mesh with 1/3 of the quads [S3, S4].
-**Strengths:** Simplicity, speed, and deterministic output. It is suitable for real-time applications where mesh generation speed is critical and mesh size is less of a concern [S3, S4].
+**Strengths:** Simplicity, speed, and deterministic output [S3, S4]. It is suitable for real-time applications where mesh generation speed is critical and mesh size is less of a concern [S3, S4].
 **Limitations:** Produces suboptimal meshes with many more triangles than Greedy Meshing. It does not merge adjacent coplanar faces, leading to higher vertex and index counts [S3, S4].
 
 ## Background
-Visible Block Faces is a fast algorithm for generating voxel block meshes, introduced as part of the `block_mesh` Rust crate [S3, S4]. It is designed to be a simple, high-performance alternative to Greedy Meshing [S3]. The method is also referenced in TypeScript implementations of voxel greedy meshing, where it is compared against Naive and Greedy meshers [S1]. A C++ implementation using Direct3D 11 was also documented, using BFS and bitmasks to track exposed faces [S2].
